      "preview_head": "of many epithelial and carcinoma cell types,[5, 6]\\(\\mathrm{TRAL}\\)-related apoptosis-inducing ligand. Oncol Immunobiology 14:153-162. One of the major goals of researchers in the field of apoptosis i",
      "preview_tail": "owever negate the utility of this molecule as a cancer therapeutic agent for tumors that did respond. ## Results ### Sensitivity and Response Mechanism Involving Tumor Necrosis Factor-Related Ligands:",
      "full_text": "of many epithelial and carcinoma cell types,[5, 6]\\(\\mathrm{TRAL}\\)-related apoptosis-inducing ligand. Oncol Immunobiology 14:153-162. One of the major goals of researchers in the field of apoptosis is to understand the molecular mechanisms by which TNF-related molecules activate caspases that cleave and degrade key enzymes, such as DNA polymerase[7] or cytoskeletal components like fodrin,[8]\\(\\mathrm{leading}\\) ultimately to cell death. In fact we have found that the TRAIL/DR5 interaction in particular activates several members of this family which lead eventually to degradation by one caspases (casp-3/casp 6, respectively[9]). It is interesting alsothat some epithelial cells respond with apoptosis while others resist TNF-related ligand mediated lysis even when similar amounts are present on the cell surfaces.[4] For instance in contrast to colon cancer and melanoma lines which appear to be quite sensitive,[1]\\({}^{\\text{--}}\\)[10] we, as well as several other groups have found that ovarian epithelial carcinoma (and normal) target populations do not express these receptor-ligands at all.[4; 11] We hypothesized therefore in this research project that there are factors produced endogenously by the ovary or those surrounding it which modulate expression of TRAIL receptors on cells. We have discovered such a molecule, interferon-\\(\\gamma\\), and demonstrated how its effect was mediated: via modulation of receptor-ligand levels.[12] Interestingly however our findings also showed that this is not always true for certain tumor types as we shall discuss below. It remains to be determined now whether soluble TRAIL or an NK lymphocyte-derived membrane bound form will actually kill a growing ovarian cancer in vivo. However the data suggest strongly such efficacy based on what has been observed with other cancers.[1]\\({}^{,}\\)[2] Nevertheless it is possible that while some tumors may respond very nicely (e.g., melanoma and colon carcinoma) others like those we worked with which are resistant even when IFN-\\(\\gamma\\)-treated,[4; 13] might still be quite impervious to TRAIL-induced apoptosis. This would not, however negate the utility of this molecule as a cancer therapeutic agent for tumors that did respond. ## Results ### Sensitivity and Response Mechanism Involving Tumor Necrosis Factor-Related Ligands:",
